    h1: 'Understanding OCD',
    lede: 'OCD is not about liking things neat. It&rsquo;s a loop: an unwanted thought that spikes real distress, and a ritual you feel forced to do to make the distress go away &mdash; which works for a moment and traps you for longer. Here&rsquo;s how it works and what helps.',
    heroCard: 'The thought is not the problem and it is not you. OCD attaches alarm to thoughts everyone has &mdash; and the alarm can be unlearned.',
    what: `<p>OCD &mdash; obsessive-compulsive disorder &mdash; has almost nothing to do with the way the word gets used in everyday speech. Being tidy, liking symmetry or being a perfectionist is not OCD. OCD is a cycle of two parts that feed each other:</p>
    <p><strong>Obsessions</strong> are unwanted, intrusive thoughts, images or urges that barge in and cause real distress &mdash; fears of contamination, of causing harm, of having done something wrong, of being a bad person, and many more. Everyone has strange intrusive thoughts; in OCD the brain tags them as dangerous and important, and they stick.</p>
    <p><strong>Compulsions</strong> are the things you do to make that distress go away &mdash; washing, checking, counting, repeating, seeking reassurance, or mental rituals nobody can see. They work for a few minutes. Then the doubt comes back, often stronger, and the ritual is needed again. That short-term relief is exactly what locks the cycle in place.</p>
    <p>The cruelty of OCD is that the compulsions feel like the solution when they are actually the fuel. The content of the obsession (germs, harm, order) matters far less than the cycle itself, which is why the same approach helps across very different fears.</p>`,
